    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-11-6-2",
    "page_path": "/explore/health/public-health/public-nutrition/",
    "name": "Public Nutrition",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/health/public-health/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Public nutrition in Iran addresses inadequate diets, micronutrient deficiencies and excess weight through health services, dietary guidance and changes to the food supply. The inspected evidence documents these overlapping concerns while leaving important gaps in comparable national monitoring. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Public nutrition", "Food security", "Child nutrition", "Dietary guidelines", "Food policy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:32:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:32:44Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Public Nutrition in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s nutrition challenges, food-based dietary guidance, child nutrition programmes and historical food reformulation policies."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "nutrition-challenges",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Overlapping forms of malnutrition",
        "content": "Public nutrition concerns the conditions that make adequate diets possible across a population. In Iran, UNICEF’s programme description identifies both insufficient nutrition and childhood excess weight, alongside deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. These problems require different measurements: a child’s weight relative to height is not a measure of micronutrient status, while excess weight does not establish that a diet supplies all necessary nutrients. The coexistence of these concerns makes a single national label such as “well nourished” misleading. [s1][s4]\n\nUNICEF identifies affordability as a barrier for vulnerable households and reports deficiencies detected in a survey of food-insecure provinces. That survey’s geographic selection matters: findings from deliberately selected vulnerable areas cannot automatically represent the whole country. UNICEF also acknowledges weaknesses in surveillance and geographic targeting. These limitations affect how confidently programmes can identify changing needs. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dietary-guidance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A food-based approach",
        "content": "The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations documents Iran’s 2015 food-based dietary guidelines, prepared under the Ministry of Health and Medical Education with academic and professional participation. Their food pyramid places bread and cereals at its base and includes vegetables, fruit, pulses, nuts, dairy foods, meat and eggs. The framework translates nutrition into recognizable foods rather than relying only on nutrient calculations. [s2]\n\nThe guidance promotes whole grains and dietary variety while calling for less salt, sugary food and sweetened drinks. It also includes hygienic preparation. Its intended audience is the general population above two years of age; it is therefore not a substitute for infant-feeding guidance or individualized clinical care. The FAO record establishes the documented guidance and its institutional origin, rather than demonstrating public adherence or confirming that no later revision exists. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "food-supply",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Changing foods as well as information",
        "content": "A 2020 study published in the Eastern Mediterranean Health Journal describes Iranian policies on salt, edible fats and nutrition labels. Its examples include bread, cheese and doogh, a fermented dairy drink. These are useful policy targets because reformulation can change the composition of familiar products without requiring consumers to replace an entire cuisine. [s3]\n\nThe study also describes traffic-light labelling, which uses colours to communicate nutrient levels. Such measures operate at a different point from dietary education: manufacturers and regulators influence what is sold, while labels help purchasers interpret products. The authors explicitly called for further research into effectiveness. Their account of historical rules should not be read as a verified statement of every legal requirement or compliance level in 2026. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "services-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Services and better evidence",
        "content": "UNICEF’s stated work includes integrating child nutrition surveillance into primary health care, improving feeding support and targeting supplementation to vulnerable groups. These are programme activities and intentions, not proof of universal access or improved outcomes. [s1]\n\nIts regional nutrition strategy also places Iran within a wider setting where undernutrition and excess weight coexist. Regional comparisons can clarify the range of problems, but their estimates must retain the original age groups and reference periods. The practical conclusion is that public nutrition needs both accessible services and a food environment that supports adequate diets; judging progress requires comparable measurements rather than combining isolated prevalence figures into an apparent trend. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {"id": "s1", "title": "Child Nutrition", "publisher": "UNICEF Iran", "url": "https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/child-nutrition", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "programme_description", "locator": "The issue faced; The actions taken; The impact sought"},
      {"id": "s2", "title": "Food-based dietary guidelines - Iran", "publisher": "Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations", "url": "https://www.fao.org/nutrition/education/dietary-guidelines/regions/iran/en/", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "national_guideline_inventory", "locator": "Publication year; Process and stakeholders; Intended audience; Food guide; Messages"},
      {"id": "s3", "title": "National food policies in the Islamic Republic of Iran aimed at control and prevention of noncommunicable diseases", "publisher": "Eastern Mediterranean Health Journal / World Health Organization", "url": "https://www.emro.who.int/emhj-volume-26-2020/volume-26-issue-12/national-food-policies-in-the-islamic-republic-of-iran-aimed-at-control-and-prevention-of-noncommunicable-diseases.html", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "research_article", "locator": "Abstract: Results and Conclusions; 2020, volume 26, issue 12, pages 1556–1564"},
      {"id": "s4", "title": "UNICEF MENA Nutrition Strategic Direction 2030 | In Brief", "publisher": "UNICEF Middle East and North Africa", "url": "https://www.unicef.org/mena/media/25911/file/Nutrition_Strategic_Brief-FINAL-18Aug24.pdf.pdf", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "regional_strategy_brief", "locator": "Printed pages 3–5, forms and determinants of malnutrition; page 8, country clusters"}
    ],
